00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:57.720
let's say, servers
in the data center,

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:00.080
how they need to
plan in advance,

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:03.680
go talk to data center
teams to get rack space,

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:06.030
to get it all wired, connected--

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.240
and that just took
so much time--

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.410
to modern ways where you can
use the power of public cloud

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:16.320
and get your VM in
less than a second.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:17.210
And it's fully there.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:18.370
It's fully up and running.

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:20.120
And it's super highly valuable.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:21.230
It's resilient.

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:22.400
It costs less.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.390
It's just mind-blowing to me.

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:26.870
And I'm happy to be
part of this journey

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:29.318
and happy to live in
such exciting times.

00:10:29.318 --> 00:10:30.860
MICHELLE CASBON: So
we've essentially

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:33.590
replaced a whole
class of problems

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:36.360
with a whole new
set of problems.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.480
So if you're not facing
those challenges anymore,

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.463
what are the new challenges
that you've been facing?

00:10:41.463 --> 00:10:43.130
YURI LITVINOVICH:
With this public cloud

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:47.390
that came to the market and
into the game, that provoked

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:51.500
big organizations like
Scotiabank to perform

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:55.190
digital transformations
because everybody understood,

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:58.160
including our leaderships,
that in this new world

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.540
where Cloud is pretty
much the only way forward,

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:05.150
we need to modernize
existing stuff.

00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:06.710
And for example,
Scotiabank-- it's

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:11.660
a huge organization with
more than 90,000 people

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:13.760
in many different countries.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:16.910
And we have 5,000-plus apps.

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:18.830
And most of these
apps were built

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:22.250
using old ways, historical
ways of building

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:25.460
applications for infrastructure
that relies on prem.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:27.290
And transition to the cloud--

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:29.420
that is a big of an effort.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.630
And this is what our
organization is doing.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:31.820
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:33.380
I want to hear more about the--

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:35.520
obviously, the cloud
details in a second.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:37.100
But I think you
kind of just touched

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:38.520
on a really interesting point.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.210
This is a big organization with
a tremendous amount of people,

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:43.220
a tremendous amount of
infrastructure and apps

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:45.180
and tooling built around those.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:46.680
You know, it's a
complicated sector.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.280
There's a ton of big
enterprises here, a lot of which

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.130
have legacy infrastructure
and architecture from past 30,

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:56.260
40, 50 years, that all of them
are facing the same problem.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:59.470
How has that journey
been for Scotiabank?

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:01.498
Like, you said the
leadership was on board.

00:12:01.498 --> 00:12:03.790
Traditionally, that's actually
not the most-- you know,

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:05.207
it's not the most
forefront thing.

00:12:05.207 --> 00:12:08.122
You don't always see
a big push to do that.

00:12:08.122 --> 00:12:09.580
So I'm really
interested in hearing

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:12.250
about how the company
has taken that response,

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.245
and how has it been so far?

00:12:14.245 --> 00:12:16.370
YURI LITVINOVICH: Yes,
that's a very good question.

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:20.200
And let me share a quote
that our CEO, Brian Porter,

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:22.840
set in 2016.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:26.170
He said that, "We are in
the technology business.

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:28.600
Our product happens
to be banking,

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:31.810
but largely that's delivered
through technology."

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:34.990
Basically, that means
that he set that direction

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:36.590
to go towards the cloud.

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:39.760
And that started the whole
chain of modernization

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:43.320
that went from top to bottom.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:45.340
And of course, funds
were allocated.

00:12:45.340 --> 00:12:48.190
We got all the talent necessary.

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:51.310
And the journey started for
us about three years ago

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:54.700
with going towards
building a platform that

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:58.060
would help developers to
accelerate their application

00:12:58.060 --> 00:12:58.727
delivery cycles.

00:12:58.727 --> 00:13:00.727
MICHELLE CASBON: I think
that's really important

00:13:00.727 --> 00:13:02.680
to have support like
that coming from the top.

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:04.055
That means that
you're not having

00:13:04.055 --> 00:13:06.970
to advocate for modernization.

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:08.950
That means that from
a business standpoint,

00:13:08.950 --> 00:13:12.130
it's seen as something
that leaders are behind.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:14.703
I think that improves
things significantly.

00:13:14.703 --> 00:13:16.120
Is that different
from what you've

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:17.940
experienced at other companies?

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:21.160
YURI LITVINOVICH: Definitely big
organizations and enterprises

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:25.450
like Scotiabank need that
push from top to bottom

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:29.260
because traditionally,
financial sector enterprises

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:32.120
are more conservative than
the rest of the industry.

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:34.600
And it's totally fair and
understandable because there

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:37.660
is lots to protect and
be conservative about.

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:40.510
Scotiabank keeps PII
data for millions

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:41.990
of people, our customers.

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:45.360
And if anything happens,
their lives might be at risk.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:47.140
All the money, let's
say, that people

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:50.030
gather for their entire
lives might be at risk.

00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:52.060
So security is definitely
a big component.

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:55.360
And that, I believe,
is what differs

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:58.180
large enterprises in financial
services like Scotiabank

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:00.260
from startups, let's say.

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:03.400
Being a huge enterprise
in financial services

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:07.060
like Scotiabank, we are
responsible for providing

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:10.390
secure transformation because
many of our customers--

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:11.930
they rely on us.

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:16.060
They rely on that change
being uninterrupted to them.

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:19.930
So clearly, having the
push from top leadership

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:21.130
is very important.

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:24.460
And that gives engineers green
light to do transformation

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:27.970
and to make sure that this
transformation will end up

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:30.670
in success because
everyone is aligned.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:33.610
While in smaller
organization, it's much easier

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:38.140
to bring bottom to top
change because it's easier

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:39.650
to pivot for them.

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:41.860
This is something that
I found in my journey

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:45.272
with both startups and
larger enterprises.

00:14:45.272 --> 00:14:46.480
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Absolutely.

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:48.410
I mean, we work with a
lot of companies out here.

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:50.710
And of course, in the Bay Area,
startups are pretty prevalent.

00:14:50.710 --> 00:14:53.410
So it's kind of easy to think
about, oh, you can kind of just

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:55.993
influence change by making
a good case for something.

00:14:55.993 --> 00:14:57.910
Well, that works really
well when your company

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:00.700
is 10 to 20 people large.

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:03.760
Turns out 90,000's a little
harder to move as quickly.

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:06.220
YURI LITVINOVICH: [LAUGHS]
You got it, exactly.

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:07.000
And you know what?

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.730
I see that's a very
natural thing that

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:10.960
happens even in the world.

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:12.760
For example, in
physics, we all know

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:15.640
that objects that
have higher mass

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:19.630
need more force, more
effort, to pivot or change

00:15:19.630 --> 00:15:21.773
direction or change velocity.

00:15:21.773 --> 00:15:23.065
Same happens with organization.

00:15:23.065 --> 00:15:25.810
The bigger the organization
is, the more effort

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:29.440
it takes to actually
pivot and transform it.

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:32.390
So it looks very,
very natural to me.

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.043
And it's just the way it works.

00:15:34.043 --> 00:15:36.460
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah, I think
that's been very noticeable

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:37.090
for me.

00:15:37.090 --> 00:15:39.750
So I didn't join a startup
until a few years ago.

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:41.170
And the first
decade of my career

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:43.390
was spent in large enterprises.

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:46.007
And I think it has less
to do with the size,

00:15:46.007 --> 00:15:47.590
like the number of
employees, and more

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:49.930
to do with the size
of the legacy software

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:51.140
that you're supporting.

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:53.920
And so, Yuri, talked
about having thousands

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:57.910
of applications that were
created in this older

00:15:57.910 --> 00:15:58.840
architectural style.

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:00.310
Those applications
weren't written overnight,

00:16:00.310 --> 00:16:02.950
which means that you're looking
at decades worth of software

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:03.280
that you're supporting.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:04.390
YURI LITVINOVICH: Absolutely.

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:05.848
MICHELLE CASBON:
And so my question

00:16:05.848 --> 00:16:08.380
to you is, how are you going
about modernizing those?

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:10.570
Is this why you chose
Kubernetes as a way

00:16:10.570 --> 00:16:13.430
to be able to modernize
all of it at once?

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:14.890
Or are you looking
at it piecemeal,

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:17.500
maybe technology type
by technology type,

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:19.550
or architecture type
by architecture type?

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:21.370
What's your approach
been in modernizing

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:23.218
that amount of legacy software?

00:16:23.218 --> 00:16:24.760
YURI LITVINOVICH:
Yeah, definitely we

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:28.150
saw the trend that
the modernization--

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:29.920
one of the pieces
of modernization

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:32.770
is going towards containers.

00:16:32.770 --> 00:16:35.410
And whole industry
goes toward containers

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:37.510
with all the benefits
that it brings,

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:41.200
including lightweight and
homogeneity during different

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:44.080
environments in dev,
in staging, in prod.

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:45.880
It's easier to test.

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:47.990
It's easier to roll out.

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:51.250
So we decided to build a
container orchestration

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:54.890
platform to help our
developers manage

00:16:54.890 --> 00:16:57.530
the lifecycle of their
applications deployed

00:16:57.530 --> 00:16:58.970
in containers.

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:02.780
And the very natural fit for
that container orchestration

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:05.162
platform was Kubernetes.

00:17:05.162 --> 00:17:07.490
It pretty much became
an industry standard

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:09.859
for this type of work.

00:17:09.859 --> 00:17:14.270
And Google, our great partner
in the digital transformation

00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:18.410
journey, has an offering of
Google Container Engine, which

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:20.369
is a managed
Kubernetes solution.

00:17:20.369 --> 00:17:23.720
So we decided to
build a platform based

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:26.480
on that, at least the
first iterations of it,

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:28.910
and basically provide
everything that it

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:32.280
brings to our end developers.

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:35.420
MICHELLE CASBON: So what
made you decide on GKE?

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:37.500
So I know that before
I joined Google,

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.740
I was in a similar
place to you where

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:42.017
I was looking at building
a platform to support

00:17:42.017 --> 00:17:43.100
containerized deployments.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:46.390
And I chose GKE, but I
was at a small startup.

00:17:46.390 --> 00:17:49.400
So series A type, 20, 30 people.

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:52.100
So I'm very interested in
what your motivation was

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:55.590
in choosing a managed Kubernetes
platform, and also why GKE,

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:58.750
and why not a managed platform
from another provider?

00:17:58.750 --> 00:18:00.860
YURI LITVINOVICH: We
decided to start with GKE

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:02.810
for a number of reasons.

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:04.970
And the first
reason is that we're

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:07.220
happy with what
GCP as a platform

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:09.560
brings to us at the bank.

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:12.770
It helped us solve many
different business challenges

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:13.820
and problems.

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:17.030
It's working well, and
our usage is increasing.

00:18:17.030 --> 00:18:20.540
And GKE was the managed
Kubernetes solution.

00:18:20.540 --> 00:18:23.675
And I know that Kubernetes--
raw, open-source Kubernetes--

00:18:23.675 --> 00:18:27.500
it takes quite a bit of effort
to configure, to manage.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:30.350
It takes quite a bit of
effort to make it work.

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:32.620
While the managed
solution from Google,

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:37.460
having a very modest price for
using that, brings lots of,

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:40.100
lots of things that
otherwise needed

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:42.810
to be developed on our own.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:44.810
So it was kind of
a natural thing

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:48.500
to try and build our
platform based on the managed

00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:49.497
solution from Google.

00:18:49.497 --> 00:18:50.330
MICHELLE CASBON: OK.

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:52.550
And what are some
of the other aspects

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:55.610
of the platform that you
like in addition to GKE?

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:58.100
Does it play nicely with
some of the other services

00:18:58.100 --> 00:18:58.967
that you use?

00:18:58.967 --> 00:19:00.300
YURI LITVINOVICH: Yeah, totally.

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:03.260
And our goal would be to
build a self-serve platform

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:06.230
for our developers
that makes it scalable.

00:19:06.230 --> 00:19:09.020
And this is just the
way to go, we believe.

00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:12.770
So having GKE Kubernetes
running on Google

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:16.380
will help to integrate
it with existing services

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:20.180
that we use from Google,
starting from compute instances

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:23.180
and custom applications
running there, and ending up

00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:27.800
with managed services like Cloud
SQL or BigQuery or Dataflow

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:29.330
or Pub/Sub.

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:32.000
They're all supported
by Stackdriver Logging

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:36.260
and Monitoring, which is just
a vital part of the whole GCP.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:39.170
That just brought so much
power into our hands.

00:19:39.170 --> 00:19:42.200
And eventually, that helped us
deliver that to our customers.

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:44.660
The same cool
infrastructure that

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:48.290
supports GKE and all other
services-- we will just

00:19:48.290 --> 00:19:51.260
make our developers
use it and have

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:52.385
all the advantages of that.

00:19:52.385 --> 00:19:54.260
MICHELLE CASBON: So it
sounds like you really

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:56.480
gravitated towards the
managed side of things

00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:58.070
rather than rolling your own.

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:01.100
Is that a new
approach, or is that

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:03.590
pretty fundamental
to Scotia's DNA?

00:20:03.590 --> 00:20:05.040
Has that been a change for you?

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:06.498
YURI LITVINOVICH:
OK, I think we're

00:20:06.498 --> 00:20:08.720
focused here more on
managed sides of things,

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:10.160
but it's actually both.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:11.930
In large enterprises
like Scotiabank,

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:15.350
we've found out that
it's just really not

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:18.800
feasible to bring
something to the plate

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:20.860
and not consider
existing infrastructure,

00:20:20.860 --> 00:20:24.470
not consider existing stuff,
not consider teams that

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:27.890
were building their, let's say,
applications or infrastructure

00:20:27.890 --> 00:20:29.880
for the last 20 years.

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:32.840
So we actually integrate
that new platform

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:34.460
with a bunch of
existing services.

00:20:34.460 --> 00:20:37.910
And I believe that was
one of the big points why

00:20:37.910 --> 00:20:40.580
the whole program
was such a success

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:43.220
and it's getting more
attention within the bank.

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:47.210
One of the reasons was because
we developed that relationship

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:48.770
with the existing services.

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:52.520
And we helped them to
modernize their stuff

00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:54.440
without any hostility of--

00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:56.910
with you guys are
old, and we are new.

00:20:56.910 --> 00:21:00.890
We are helping them to get
all the benefits of Cloud

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:03.900
and doing that with
their own pace.

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:05.750
Let's say they
want to slowly move

00:21:05.750 --> 00:21:07.430
some workloads to the cloud.

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:08.960
Sure, it's totally use case.

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:10.250
It's fair.

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:11.900
And we help them to do that.

00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:14.420
If some people
want to go all in,

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:16.040
we have applications like that.

00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:18.320
Cloud is also
available for that.

00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:21.530
So definitely on top of
having that managed services

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:25.790
from Google Cloud Platform, we
build a lot of stuff around it

00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:27.710
to make it usable for
the bank and to make

00:21:27.710 --> 00:21:30.830
it compatible with existing
security processes,

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:34.770
with existing operational
readiness processes.

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:37.023
And that's what made
quite a success.

00:21:37.023 --> 00:21:39.440
MICHELLE CASBON: So it sounds
like you had the flexibility

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:41.210
that you needed to
choose manage services

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:44.600
where it made sense, but not
in a way that forced people

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:45.270
to use them.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:48.110
So you focused on building
a self-service platform that

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:50.780
empowered people without
strong-arming them

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:53.060
or having to these
blanket policies

00:21:53.060 --> 00:21:56.150
for this entire giant
organization to follow.

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:57.650
YURI LITVINOVICH: Right, right.

00:21:57.650 --> 00:21:58.170
Definitely.

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:01.160
And that is being
accepted very positively

00:22:01.160 --> 00:22:04.100
because people understand
that we are there to help.

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:06.950
We're there to help them
solve their business

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:11.480
problems with less resources,
with more cost efficient,

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:14.570
with better technology, and
is definitely considered

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:15.800
a very positive change.

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:17.960
So people co-create with us.

00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:20.250
And this is something
that we value.

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:21.080
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
when working with all

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:22.038
these different teams--

00:22:22.038 --> 00:22:25.400
I mean, you've kind of alluded
to the different parts of GCP

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:28.280
that you use to make their
migration a lot easier.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:31.340
What's been your favorite
part or the part that

00:22:31.340 --> 00:22:35.030
has been the most helpful in
convincing a lot of these teams

00:22:35.030 --> 00:22:37.500
that that migration is possible?

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:39.290
YURI LITVINOVICH: One
of the best benefits

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:43.250
that you can get by
going to public cloud

00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:47.330
is not only cost
efficiency, but a bunch

00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:51.860
of things that are traditionally
very hard to achieve.

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:54.050
For example, it's
instant provisioning.

00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:56.000
You don't need to
wait for hardware

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:58.760
to be ordered and
set up and configured

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:00.050
and then ready for you.

00:23:00.050 --> 00:23:02.840
I would say dynamic
provisioning is a great one

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:05.800
as well because we only
pay for what we use.

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:07.550
And when you don't
need it, you can

00:23:07.550 --> 00:23:09.950
configure Cloud and
services around it

00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:13.700
to automatically scale
down and take care of that.

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:18.980
And of course, the resources are
nearly unlimited in the cloud.

00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:22.550
You don't have to worry about
you running out of disk space,

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:25.070
let's say, or running
out of compute power.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:26.330
Just get more instances.

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:27.950
Just get more storage.

00:23:27.950 --> 00:23:30.410
And I also believe that
Cloud can provide you

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:33.890
with more enhanced
security than what

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:35.180
you can achieve on your own.

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:37.520
Because cloud providers--
they can afford--

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:40.580
have thousands of security
architects and specialists

00:23:40.580 --> 00:23:42.620
that can reach the
level of security

00:23:42.620 --> 00:23:45.440
that hardly even the
big organizations

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:49.530
can afford if they have
their dedicated teams.

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:52.010
So if properly
engineered, public cloud

00:23:52.010 --> 00:23:55.070
[? can ?] be made safer
than your own dedicated

00:23:55.070 --> 00:23:56.270
infrastructure.

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:59.510
And you don't pay any upfront
costs on using the cloud.

00:23:59.510 --> 00:24:01.910
As I said, you pay
for only you use.

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:05.060
And this has a lot of
benefits and convincing points

00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:08.360
for them to start
that modernization

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:10.043
and join this
transformation happening.

00:24:10.043 --> 00:24:11.960
MICHELLE CASBON: And
that's really interesting

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:15.605
to hear you highlight enhanced
security as a benefit of cloud

00:24:15.605 --> 00:24:17.480
because I think a lot
of people traditionally

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:21.590
are scared of cloud because they
perceive it to be less secure.

00:24:21.590 --> 00:24:23.450
Could you just
share maybe a couple

00:24:23.450 --> 00:24:26.540
of your favorite security
enhancements, or just some tips

00:24:26.540 --> 00:24:29.600
for people who are looking to
make their apps more secure?

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:31.960
What have you found most useful?

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:35.480
YURI LITVINOVICH: Right, so
one of the first and grounding

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:39.320
and the very base thing that
GCP offers is total encryption.

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:41.480
Everything is encrypted at REST.

00:24:41.480 --> 00:24:43.710
Encryption [? is ?]
[? intrinsic ?] [? is ?] there.

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:46.210
So it's very hard for
some malicious individual,

00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:49.730
let's say, in a data
center, who are secure too,

00:24:49.730 --> 00:24:53.390
get any information, even if
they have access to hardware.

00:24:53.390 --> 00:24:56.870
So another great thing that
you can achieve with Cloud

00:24:56.870 --> 00:25:00.650
is having your
environments configured

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:02.180
in a very similar way.

00:25:02.180 --> 00:25:05.520
And here is a great
use case for Anthos,

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:07.790
the new thing that
Google introduced quite

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:10.680
recently at Next this year.

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:13.580
This can be used as a powerful
configuration management

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:19.040
tool that can span not only for
GKE, but also for GKE On-Prem.

00:25:19.040 --> 00:25:22.250
And I believe there are
plans to support other cloud

00:25:22.250 --> 00:25:26.000
providers like Amazon or Azure.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:28.520
And having that
one central place

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:31.490
where you control your
configuration and security

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:34.560
and other things is
just a no-brainer

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:36.710
that this is more
secure than when

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:39.680
you have multiple environments
and maybe multiple people

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:42.410
responsible for configuring
each environment.

00:25:42.410 --> 00:25:45.710
I would say this is one of
the good points of having

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:47.913
that enhanced security
that comes with Cloud.

00:25:47.913 --> 00:25:50.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So with all
of the security in the cloud

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:52.080
and all these other
advantages that you've

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:54.050
been able to convince
your teams of, hey,

00:25:54.050 --> 00:25:55.740
these are good
reasons to migrate,

00:25:55.740 --> 00:25:58.340
I'm also interested in
hearing what their number one

00:25:58.340 --> 00:26:00.445
concern was to not migrate.

00:26:00.445 --> 00:26:01.820
So what kind of
pushback have you

00:26:01.820 --> 00:26:04.340
been getting from all
of these instances that

00:26:04.340 --> 00:26:05.967
have been challenging for you?

00:26:05.967 --> 00:26:08.300
Because obviously, there are
going to be a lot of issues

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:09.620
when planning a migration.

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:12.260
YURI LITVINOVICH: I believe one
of the challenges that we've

00:26:12.260 --> 00:26:14.690
encountered was trying
to convince them

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:17.840
that having to modernize
their application

00:26:17.840 --> 00:26:21.080
is the effort that
they need to perform

00:26:21.080 --> 00:26:22.910
and it will benefit them.

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:25.400
Because going to
Cloud, we promote

00:26:25.400 --> 00:26:29.180
when you actually modernize
your application, right?

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:30.770
You don't just lift and shift.

00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:33.380
Otherwise, Cloud becomes
just another data center

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:35.420
for you, just in a
different location.

00:26:35.420 --> 00:26:38.210
We really promote you to build
your application using all

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:40.500
the DevOps principles in mind.

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:42.440
And that's required
a bit of work.

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:44.780
So I believe one
of the challenges

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:47.870
that sometimes we have
with application team

00:26:47.870 --> 00:26:50.510
is to actually show them
that Cloud is faster,

00:26:50.510 --> 00:26:55.250
it's more cost effective, and
it will deliver value faster.

00:26:55.250 --> 00:26:58.640
But I would say those are the
early days of the program, when

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:00.890
the thing was just
introduced to Scotiabank

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:04.580
and no one really heard
about doing public cloud

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:06.620
for financial services.

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:09.450
Everyone was like, OK,
cool, another new feature

00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:10.310
or something.

00:27:10.310 --> 00:27:13.670
But slowly, gradually,
we build the trust

00:27:13.670 --> 00:27:17.270
by having success stories from
other application teams that

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:20.390
took a risk allocating
resources to actually do

00:27:20.390 --> 00:27:22.530
the modernization
and go to the cloud.

00:27:22.530 --> 00:27:26.600
And once application teams see
the velocity that it brings--

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:31.850
so for example, used to take
six months for some applications

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:34.220
and teams to get
their data centers

00:27:34.220 --> 00:27:37.730
set up, VMs, infrastructure,
then another month

00:27:37.730 --> 00:27:39.380
to get databases.

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:41.930
That was reduced
to, maximum, hours.

00:27:41.930 --> 00:27:43.700
And I think it's a no-brainer.

00:27:43.700 --> 00:27:45.440
Which one would you prefer?

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:48.170
Besides, having
security on board,

00:27:48.170 --> 00:27:50.540
you get all the same
sign-off from bank security

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:52.580
team that says, OK,
this is trusted,

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:53.840
and it's been verified.

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:56.340
MICHELLE CASBON: So that brings
up a really important point,

00:27:56.340 --> 00:27:57.920
because finance--

00:27:57.920 --> 00:27:59.870
and not just finance,
but retail finance--

00:27:59.870 --> 00:28:02.462
is such a highly
regulated industry.

00:28:02.462 --> 00:28:04.920
What are some of the things
that are most important to you?

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:07.850
What gets you that
sign-off from other teams

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:10.550
to let you move
applications into the cloud?

00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:12.050
What was really crucial for you?

00:28:12.050 --> 00:28:13.675
YURI LITVINOVICH:
The good thing that I

00:28:13.675 --> 00:28:16.530
can share with our audience
from my experience working

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:20.450
in financial sector is that
you need to build trust

00:28:20.450 --> 00:28:22.040
with other teams.

00:28:22.040 --> 00:28:25.270
You need to prove that you
are a friend to security,

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:28.070
that you are a friend to
operational readiness team,

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:31.370
or whoever is responsible,
to go into production.

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:33.230
You need to build
this relationship

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:36.050
to help them understand
stuff, to promote

00:28:36.050 --> 00:28:39.680
that friendship instead of
maybe hostility, and sometimes

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:41.420
positioning yourself
of, oh, we're

00:28:41.420 --> 00:28:44.780
doing the modern cutting-edge
technology stuff, versus you

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:47.930
guys are too
conservative, too old, too

00:28:47.930 --> 00:28:49.610
concerned about everything.

00:28:49.610 --> 00:28:51.680
Help them understand
what you are doing.

00:28:51.680 --> 00:28:54.450
Because the truth is
Cloud is the way to go.

00:28:54.450 --> 00:28:57.680
The truth is companies are
going there for a reason--

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:00.080
because it's better
in so many ways.

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:04.220
It's just a matter of patiently
explaining and sometimes

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:06.170
spending extra
time to help people

00:29:06.170 --> 00:29:08.190
understand what you are doing.

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:12.080
And from my experience, that
pays off a hundred times.

00:29:12.080 --> 00:29:14.180
You get your sign-offs faster.

00:29:14.180 --> 00:29:15.710
You get people up to speed.

00:29:15.710 --> 00:29:17.390
And you just spread
the word, spread

00:29:17.390 --> 00:29:19.520
the knowledge of the
transformation that's

00:29:19.520 --> 00:29:20.120
happening.

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:21.662
MICHELLE CASBON: So
is it fair to say

00:29:21.662 --> 00:29:24.750
that many of the challenges have
not been technical in nature--

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:28.305
they've been more
human in nature?

00:29:28.305 --> 00:29:29.930
YURI LITVINOVICH:
Yeah, I would say so.

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:30.890
It's a bit of both.

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:32.760
Of course, there is a
technical piece of it.

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:35.913
But there is also a human
aspect in transformation.

00:29:35.913 --> 00:29:36.830
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah.

00:29:36.830 --> 00:29:38.330
I'm very familiar
with that problem.

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:42.405
I think most engineers I spend
a good portion of their time

00:29:42.405 --> 00:29:44.030
really tackling the
human issues rather

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:45.280
than the technical challenges.

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:47.697
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Again, you
can't have somebody just show

00:29:47.697 --> 00:29:49.850
up to a company with a big
picket whiteboard fence

00:29:49.850 --> 00:29:51.267
and say like, hey,
let's go Cloud.

00:29:51.267 --> 00:29:53.058
And then everyone's
like, oh, that's great.

00:29:53.058 --> 00:29:54.033
Let's do it.

00:29:54.033 --> 00:29:55.700
It's not going to
work like that, right?

00:29:55.700 --> 00:29:57.208
So what would you
say the best piece

00:29:57.208 --> 00:29:59.750
of advice that you could pass
on or the things that you found

00:29:59.750 --> 00:30:02.270
to be most helpful when
you started this journey

00:30:02.270 --> 00:30:05.030
or when this journey was being
started, to say, how do we

00:30:05.030 --> 00:30:05.990
get things running?

00:30:05.990 --> 00:30:08.157
YURI LITVINOVICH: One of
the advices that I can give

00:30:08.157 --> 00:30:11.690
would be to outline the
benefits of the transformation

00:30:11.690 --> 00:30:14.750
that you want to
do very clearly.

00:30:14.750 --> 00:30:17.630
Make sure you understand
what you want to bring.

00:30:17.630 --> 00:30:19.370
And make sure that
people who are

00:30:19.370 --> 00:30:22.790
responsible for decision-making
are also on board

00:30:22.790 --> 00:30:25.310
and they understand
why you are trying

00:30:25.310 --> 00:30:26.720
to do the transformation.

00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:29.330
Because it's quite
obvious that many things

00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:31.700
about going Cloud
and going Kubernetes,

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:35.990
going all the cool tools like
Anthos, Istio are better.

00:30:35.990 --> 00:30:37.070
Show that.

00:30:37.070 --> 00:30:38.870
Prove that this is
indeed the case.

00:30:38.870 --> 00:30:42.710
That might require a bit of
extra time in building a POC.

00:30:42.710 --> 00:30:45.560
But to have something
tangible to say.

00:30:45.560 --> 00:30:47.505
And those arguments
are really convincing.

00:30:47.505 --> 00:30:49.070
So that would be one.

00:30:49.070 --> 00:30:52.010
And one of the other
tips that can be shared

00:30:52.010 --> 00:30:55.130
is that, look at the
success of other companies,

00:30:55.130 --> 00:30:58.130
success of other people who
did that transformation,

00:30:58.130 --> 00:31:01.700
and show that this is real, that
this is something that already

00:31:01.700 --> 00:31:05.390
happened, and you are not the
first one who is doing that.

00:31:05.390 --> 00:31:08.390
Because Cloud has been
happening for quite a while now,

00:31:08.390 --> 00:31:12.000
though it's still very
hot and very exciting.

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:15.180
And there are good examples
of good success stories

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:18.920
of going to the cloud,
using Kubernetes, using

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:20.613
other cool technologies.

00:31:20.613 --> 00:31:21.530
So it's already there.

00:31:21.530 --> 00:31:24.250
And that makes it easier
for those people who

00:31:24.250 --> 00:31:27.470
have funds and make
decisions to make

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:29.872
decisions towards your points.

00:31:29.872 --> 00:31:32.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So to your
first point about maybe taking

00:31:32.330 --> 00:31:35.060
some extra time and actually
showing the benefits, have you

00:31:35.060 --> 00:31:37.640
found things like
Kubernetes, Istio, Anthos,

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:41.600
all of those a good platform for
maybe setting up either demos

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:44.692
or proving the technology
to help the cause?

00:31:44.692 --> 00:31:46.400
YURI LITVINOVICH:
Absolutely, absolutely.

00:31:46.400 --> 00:31:49.910
We did that within
Scotiabank, within PLATO,

00:31:49.910 --> 00:31:51.230
within our customers.

00:31:51.230 --> 00:31:54.050
And we showed them
that apart from having

00:31:54.050 --> 00:31:56.735
the power of all the
underlying technologies,

00:31:56.735 --> 00:31:58.850
it creates this velocity.

00:31:58.850 --> 00:32:02.990
It brings more stability
and more resiliency, cost

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:06.290
efficiency, and it's tangible.

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:07.570
It's easy to use.

00:32:07.570 --> 00:32:09.237
MICHELLE CASBON: And
so I think velocity

00:32:09.237 --> 00:32:10.910
is really key in
highlighting some

00:32:10.910 --> 00:32:12.830
of the benefits of
these technologies.

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:14.510
Is there anything
that it's enabled

00:32:14.510 --> 00:32:18.200
you to do that you just couldn't
have done before even at a very

00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:19.190
slow velocity?

00:32:19.190 --> 00:32:20.690
Has it really changed
things for you

00:32:20.690 --> 00:32:22.620
fundamentally as
an organization?

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:24.370
YURI LITVINOVICH: Good
question, Michelle.

00:32:24.370 --> 00:32:25.130
[CHUCKLES]

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:27.620
MICHELLE CASBON: How has
it changed your life, Yuri?

00:32:27.620 --> 00:32:29.120
YURI LITVINOVICH:
I'm still excited.

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:30.860
I'm still excited
about the cloud.

00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:33.470
I'm still excited about
the transformation,

00:32:33.470 --> 00:32:35.970
though I've been working
for years in that field.

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:37.673
But I still see
new things coming.

00:32:37.673 --> 00:32:39.590
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I mean,
Kubernetes is great.

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:41.510
But it hasn't made my
commute shorter yet.

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:42.590
YURI LITVINOVICH:
[LAUGHS] I see.

00:32:42.590 --> 00:32:44.090
MARK MIRCHANDANI: But
hasn't it, though?

00:32:44.090 --> 00:32:46.740
I mean, if you think about these
large-scale infrastructures

00:32:46.740 --> 00:32:48.120
that might be running,
like the train system

00:32:48.120 --> 00:32:50.150
might be running on
Cloud, that might actually

00:32:50.150 --> 00:32:51.200
make my commute shorter.

00:32:51.200 --> 00:32:52.658
MICHELLE CASBON:
Before we know it,

00:32:52.658 --> 00:32:54.350
[? Muni ?] might
completely reconstruct

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:57.440
their entire infrastructure,
